label: Measure Soil Polygon
description: >-
  Area of one clipped segment, in acres.

  ##### THE FINDING OF THIS WHOLE SLICE #####

  In blue-ribbon this is not code. It is a SQL string constant:

    const AREA_ACRES_SQL = `ST_Area(geom) * POWER(111320.0, 2)
      * COS(RADIANS(ST_Y(ST_Centroid(geom)))) / 4046.8564224`;

  interpolated into a query and executed by DuckDB's spatial extension
  (duckdb/compute.ts:12). Its own comment says it is "NOT ST_Area_Spheroid or
  an ST_Transform-based area, both proven wrong by ~8.4x at this latitude
  during spec verification."

  So: the single most error-prone number on the card, wrong by 8.4x twice,
  has no unit test -- because you cannot unit-test a SQL fragment without
  standing up a database and loading geometry into it. The nondeterminism
  budget of this function is ZERO. It is shoelace area times two constants
  times a cosine. It is the most testable thing in the entire repo and it is
  the least tested.

  Here it is a pure node over declared structure, with properties. That is a
  real improvement and it is not the improvement I predicted -- I predicted
  weir would be a shell describing the least interesting part. It went the
  other way: the shell reached INTO the part that mattered and pulled it out
  of the database.

  WHAT DUCKDB IS ACTUALLY NEEDED FOR, once this is done: nothing, in this
  path. The clip is server-side at SDA. The area is arithmetic. The spatial
  engine in the A2 pipeline is doing work that does not require a spatial
  engine. (ST_Buffer for corridor selection is genuinely spatial -- but that
  is universe selection, upstream, once per corridor, not per parcel.)
input: SoilPolygonShape
output: SoilPolygonArea
examples:
  - given:
      SoilPolygonShape:
        polygonId: "sp-1"
        mukey: "162341"
        rings:
          "0":
            seq: 0
            vertices:
              "0": { seq: 0, lng: -85.3, lat: 44 }
              "1": { seq: 1, lng: -85.3, lat: 44.001 }
              "2": { seq: 2, lng: -85.299, lat: 44.001 }
              "3": { seq: 3, lng: -85.299, lat: 44 }
              "4": { seq: 4, lng: -85.3, lat: 44 }
    expect:
      SoilPolygonArea:
        polygonId: "sp-1"
        mukey: "162341"
        acres: 2.202718
properties:
  - name: area is never negative
    description: >-
      Shoelace returns a signed area whose sign is ring winding order. SDA does
      not guarantee winding. An implementation that forgets the absolute value
      returns negative acreage for every clockwise ring -- which is exactly the
      class of bug that produced the 8.4x error, and which nothing in
      blue-ribbon would currently catch until validateCard rejected the card
      several nodes later with no idea which polygon caused it.
    expr:
      gte:
        - get: output.acres
        - lit: 0
  - name: the segment keeps its identity through measurement
    description: Measuring must not re-key a polygon onto a different map unit.
    expr:
      and:
        - eq:
            - get: output.polygonId
            - get: input.polygonId
        - eq:
            - get: output.mukey
            - get: input.mukey
